/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Start with dU = T dS − p dV.
   - The left side is the rate of change of temperature with volume at constant entropy; the right side is minus the rate of change of pressure with entropy at constant volume.
   - That is the first Maxwell relation.
   - The last two hold T fixed and put entropy on the left, so they are the working relations: (∂s/∂v)T = (∂p/∂T)v and (∂s/∂p)T = −(∂v/∂T)p, per unit mass.
   - Figure 4.5.1 shows the A relation at work for carbon dioxide: the slope of each constant-volume line on a p–T diagram is the rate at which entropy grows with volume at that temperature.
   - Write s = s(T, v) and s = s(T, p), use cv = T(∂s/∂T)v and cp = T(∂s/∂T)p, and replace the entropy slopes by Maxwell relations.
   - T ds = cvdT + T(∂p/∂T)vdv
   - First TdS equation: cv in kJ/(kg·K); the slope (∂p/∂T)v comes from the equation of state.
   - T ds = cpdT − T(∂v/∂T)pdp
   - Second TdS equation: cp in kJ/(kg·K); the slope (∂v/∂T)p is measured at constant pressure.
*/
