/* book Worked example: Q: Air (ideal gas, R = 0.287 kJ/(kg·K)) is compressed isothermally at 300 K from 1 bar to 5 bar. Use a Maxwell relation to find the entropy change per kg.
   1. The slope needed links s, p and T, so use the G relation: (∂s/∂p)T = −(∂v/∂T)p.
   2. For an ideal gas v = RT/p, so (∂v/∂T)p = R/p.
   3. Hence (∂s/∂p)T = −R/p, and at constant T, ds = −R dp/p.
   4. Integrate from 1 bar to 5 bar: Δs = −R·ln(p₂/p₁) = −0.287 × ln 5 = −0.287 × 1.6094 = −0.4619 kJ/(kg·K).
   5. The sign is negative: squeezing the gas into a smaller volume at constant temperature lowers its entropy.
   Answer: Δs = −0.462 kJ/(kg·K). */
