/* book Worked example 2: Q: 1 mol of carbon dioxide, treated as a van der Waals gas with a = 0.364 Pa·m⁶/mol² and b = 4.27 × 10⁻⁵ m³/mol, expands isothermally at 300 K from 0.001 to 0.002 m³/mol. Use Maxwell relations to find Δs and Δu, and compare Δs with the ideal-gas value. R = 8.314 J/(mol·K).
   1. Equation of state: p = RT/(v − b) − a/v². At constant v: (∂p/∂T)v = R/(v − b).
   2. A relation: (∂s/∂v)T = (∂p/∂T)v = R/(v − b).
   3. Integrate at constant T: Δs = R·ln[(v₂ − b)/(v₁ − b)] = 8.314 × ln(0.0019573/0.0009573) = 8.314 × ln 2.0446 = 8.314 × 0.7152 = 5.946 J/(mol·K).
   4. Ideal gas: Δs = R·ln(v₂/v₁) = 8.314 × 0.6931 = 5.763 J/(mol·K). The real gas gains about 3.2 % more, because the molecules' own volume b takes up part of the space.
   5. Energy relation: (∂u/∂v)T = T(∂p/∂T)v − p = RT/(v − b) − [RT/(v − b) − a/v²] = a/v².
   6. Integrate: Δu = a(1/v₁ − 1/v₂) = 0.364 × (1000 − 500) = 182 J/mol.
   Answer: Δs = 5.946 J/(mol·K), against 5.763 J/(mol·K) for an ideal gas, and Δu = +182 J/mol: the gas stores energy as its molecules move apart against their attraction, where an ideal gas would have Δu = 0. */
