/* book Worked example: Q: Water boils at T₁ = 373 K where p₁ = 101.3 kPa. Take hfg = 2257 kJ/kg and R = 0.4615 kJ/(kg·K) for steam. With hfg held constant, estimate the saturation pressure at T₂ = 323 K, and explain why it differs from the steam-table value of 12.35 kPa.
   1. Both temperatures are already in kelvin.
   2. 1/T₂ = 1/323 = 3.0960 × 10⁻³ K⁻¹ and 1/T₁ = 1/373 = 2.6810 × 10⁻³ K⁻¹; the difference is 4.150 × 10⁻⁴ K⁻¹.
   3. hfg/R = 2257/0.4615 = 4890.6 K.
   4. Exponent: −4890.6 × 4.150 × 10⁻⁴ = −2.0296.
   5. p₂/p₁ = e−2.0296 = 0.13138, so p₂ = 101.3 × 0.13138 = 13.31 kPa.
   6. The estimate is about 8 % above 12.35 kPa. The real hfg rises from 2257 kJ/kg at 373 K to about 2382 kJ/kg at 323 K; using the larger value over part of the range would make the exponent more negative and the pressure lower. The constant-hfg assumption is the main error over a 50 K gap.
   Answer: The estimate is 13.3 kPa, against 12.35 kPa in the steam tables; the gap comes mainly from holding hfg constant. */
