/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - β is the volume expansivity (coefficient of volume expansion), in 1/K: the fractional increase of volume per kelvin at constant pressure.
   - κT is the isothermal compressibility, in 1/Pa: the fractional decrease of volume per pascal at constant temperature.
   - κs is the isentropic (adiabatic) compressibility; it sets the speed of sound, c = √(v/κs).
   - For an ideal gas, v = RT/p gives β = 1/T, κT = 1/p and κs = 1/(γp), where γ = cp/cv.
   - The cyclic relation links the two coefficients to the pressure slope: (∂p/∂T)v = −(∂v/∂T)p/(∂v/∂p)T = β/κT.
   - cp − cv = Tvβ²/κT
   - With T in K, v in m³/kg, β in 1/K and κT in 1/Pa, the result is in J/(kg·K).
   - A throttle (a valve or porous plug) is adiabatic, does no work and leaves the kinetic energy nearly unchanged, so h₁ = h₂: throttling is a constant-enthalpy process.
   - μJT is in K/Pa, or K/bar in practice.
   - Set dh = 0 in dh = cpdT + [v − T(∂v/∂T)p]dp and solve for dT/dp:
*/
