/* book Worked example: Q: For liquid water at 25 °C (298.15 K), v = 0.001003 m³/kg, β = 2.57 × 10⁻⁴ K⁻¹, κT = 4.52 × 10⁻¹⁰ Pa⁻¹ and cp = 4.18 kJ/(kg·K). Find cp − cv, cv and γ, and the pressure rise per kelvin if the water is heated in a full, rigid, sealed vessel.
   1. β² = (2.57 × 10⁻⁴)² = 6.605 × 10⁻⁸ K⁻².
   2. Tvβ² = 298.15 × 0.001003 × 6.605 × 10⁻⁸ = 1.975 × 10⁻⁸ m³·K⁻¹/kg.
   3. cp − cv = 1.975 × 10⁻⁸/4.52 × 10⁻¹⁰ = 43.7 J/(kg·K) = 0.0437 kJ/(kg·K).
   4. cv = 4.18 − 0.0437 = 4.136 kJ/(kg·K), and γ = 4.18/4.136 = 1.011.
   5. Rigid vessel: (∂p/∂T)v = β/κT = 2.57 × 10⁻⁴/4.52 × 10⁻¹⁰ = 5.69 × 10⁵ Pa/K, about 5.7 bar per kelvin.
   Answer: cp − cv = 0.0437 kJ/(kg·K), about 1 % of cp; cv = 4.136 kJ/(kg·K); γ = 1.011; the sealed vessel gains about 5.7 bar per kelvin. */
