/* book Worked example 2: Q: Nitrogen at 300 K is throttled from 20 bar to 1 bar. Estimate its temperature change with the low-pressure van der Waals result μJT ≈ (2a/RT − b)/cp,m, taking a = 0.137 Pa·m⁶/mol², b = 3.87 × 10⁻⁵ m³/mol, cp,m = 29.1 J/(mol·K) and R = 8.314 J/(mol·K). Also estimate the inversion temperature.
   1. 2a/(RT) = 2 × 0.137/(8.314 × 300) = 0.274/2494.2 = 1.0986 × 10⁻⁴ m³/mol.
   2. 2a/(RT) − b = 1.0986 × 10⁻⁴ − 0.387 × 10⁻⁴ = 7.116 × 10⁻⁵ m³/mol.
   3. μJT = 7.116 × 10⁻⁵/29.1 = 2.445 × 10⁻⁶ K/Pa = 0.2445 K/bar (1 bar = 10⁵ Pa).
   4. ΔT ≈ μJTΔp = 0.2445 × (1 − 20) = −4.6 K, so the gas leaves at about 295.4 K.
   5. Inversion temperature: Ti = 2a/(Rb) = 0.274/(8.314 × 3.87 × 10⁻⁵) = 0.274/(3.218 × 10⁻⁴) = 852 K. At 300 K nitrogen is far below it, so μJT is positive and it cools.
   Answer: μJT ≈ 0.245 K/bar, so the nitrogen cools by about 4.6 K, to about 295.4 K. The van der Waals inversion temperature is about 852 K; the measured maximum is lower, near 620 K. */
